WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.214
Fornax runs open models in your JVM.

00:00:03.214 --> 00:00:07.408
For business, industrial,
medical and creative work,

00:00:07.408 --> 00:00:11.199
or anywhere a specialized
model earns its keep.

00:00:11.199 --> 00:00:13.595
Plain Java calls, on your GPU.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:20.103
If you want a local language model
or embeddings in Java, there are options.

00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:24.142
They work, though they're often
slower than they should be.

00:00:24.142 --> 00:00:28.455
But what if you want to flag
a fracture on an X-ray for review?

00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:30.783
Spot defects on a production line?

00:00:30.783 --> 00:00:32.494
Read a stack of invoices?

00:00:32.494 --> 00:00:34.685
Isolate the vocals from a track?

00:00:34.685 --> 00:00:37.629
Or any of a plethora
of other capabilities?

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:40.367
Until now, from the JVM, you were stuck.

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:45.680
It meant a Python stack, or a tool like
ComfyUI behind HTTP: another runtime,

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.062
another process,
and a network hop on every call.

00:00:49.062 --> 00:00:51.732
Fornax calls the native engines directly.

00:00:51.732 --> 00:00:54.539
No Python, no server, nothing in between.

00:00:55.833 --> 00:00:59.029
It uses Java's Foreign
Function and Memory API,

00:00:59.029 --> 00:01:02.496
so the call into the engine
is a plain native call.

00:01:02.496 --> 00:01:06.226
Weights load once and stay
on the GPU between requests,

00:01:06.226 --> 00:01:11.583
and when memory runs short, Fornax unloads
the models you've used least recently.

00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:15.958
Text streams back token by token,
and models can call your tools.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:22.815
Try a model in Fornax Studio before you
write any code: read an invoice into JSON,

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:27.267
spot a defect on a part, or flag
an X-ray for a doctor's review.

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:31.136
Or chat, paint an image,
write a song, or render a clip.

00:01:31.136 --> 00:01:36.652
On our test laptop, Fornax made a one-minute
song up to twice as fast as ComfyUI,

00:01:36.652 --> 00:01:37.769
on the same GPU.

00:01:39.066 --> 00:01:43.967
Pull a model from Hugging Face with
one command, and try it in Fornax Studio.

00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:47.938
Fornax needs no other Java libraries,
just its native engines,

00:01:47.938 --> 00:01:53.431
so it works in any app, and it's a great
companion for Embabel Agent: local models,

00:01:53.431 --> 00:01:54.160
in process.

00:01:55.466 --> 00:01:59.075
Here's what open models
can make, running in Fornax.

00:01:59.075 --> 00:02:01.746
This is Endless Country, episode two.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:11.488
Fornax!

00:02:11.488 --> 00:02:14.492
Open models, inside your JVM!

00:02:14.492 --> 00:02:20.087
Sponsored by Rod Johnson,
and created by Jasper Blues!

00:02:20.087 --> 00:02:20.812
fornax4j.

00:02:20.812 --> 00:02:21.227
org!
